\n\n4. **Lines 425–488** — The code manipulates the membership of **`$user`** (the victim loaded from the attacker-controlled UUID): signing them up, cancelling them, setting them as tentative, modifying their comment/guest count.\n\n**Result:** A regular authenticated user can forge a POST request (with a valid CSRF token obtained from their own participation form) and manipulate **any other user's** event participation — enrolling them against their will, cancelling their registration, etc.\n\n### The fix\n\nThe after-version inserts:\n```php\n// if user is no leader of the event then only allow to handle their own participation\nif (!$participants->isLeader($gCurrentUserId)) {\n    $getUserUuid = $gCurrentUser->getValue('usr_uuid');\n}\n```\n\nThis enforces that non-leaders can only act on their own user record. Leaders (who are already trusted to manage participants) can still use the `user_uuid` parameter to manage other users.\n\n### CWE Classification\n\nThis is a classic **Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR)** — the application exposes a direct reference to an internal object (a user, via their UUID) and fails to verify that the requesting user is authorized to perform actions on that object. The most specific CWE is:\n\n- **CWE-639: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key** — A user-controlled key (`user_uuid`) is used to directly access a user's participation data without proper authorization verification.\n\nWhile CWE-862 (Missing Authorization) broadly applies, CWE-639 is the more precise fit because the root cause is specifically the user-controlled identifier that grants access to another user's data.\n\n---\n\nVULNERABILITY_FOUND: yes\nCWE_ID: CWE-639\nCWE_NAME: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key\n", "output_dir": "/home/medo/laMiaTesi/agentAnalysisDocker/outputs/polito_deepseek-v4/Admidio__admidio/e47f70cc3cbc/agent2", "created_at": "2026-08-07T07:59:25.733511+00:00"}
