rated in the new test `test_httpbin_mock_redirect()` (file 11, line 143-173) which explicitly tests: initial URL `https://httpbin.org/redirect-to?url=http://127.0.0.1/secret` → redirect to `http://127.0.0.1/secret` → **blocked** by the new code.\n\n**2. SSRF via Encoded IP Addresses**\n\nThe old `validate_import_url()` did not detect non-standard IP representations that some systems can resolve:\n- **DWORD**: `2130706433` = `127.0.0.1`\n- **Hex**: `0x7f000001` = `127.0.0.1`\n- **Octal**: `017700000001` = `127.0.0.1`\n\nThese formats can bypass URL validators and DNS resolution in unexpected ways across different operating systems and libraries.\n\n### The Fix\n\nThe commit introduces `safe_request()` (file_01) which:\n1. Sets `allow_redirects=False` on every underlying `requests.request()` call\n2. Manually follows redirects one at a time in a loop (max 10)\n3. Calls `validate_import_url()` on **every** URL (initial + every redirect hop)\n4. Handles relative redirect URLs correctly\n5. Detects circular redirects\n\nAdditionally, `validate_import_url()` is enhanced to:\n- Reject control characters in URLs\n- Normalize URLs (encode spaces/special chars for the validator)\n- Detect and decode encoded IP addresses (DWORD, hex, octal) and check them against private/reserved ranges\n\nAll 26+ instances of direct `requests.get()` / `requests.post()` across the codebase (integration files, providers, views, Telegram bot) are replaced with `safe_request()`.\n\n### Classification\n\nThe vulnerability is **Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)** — CWE-918. This is the most specific and accurate classification. The redirect-following behavior is specifically a form of SSRF where the attacker uses HTTP redirects as the bypass mechanism, but the underlying weakness class is SSRF.\n\n---\n\nVULNERABILITY_FOUND: yes\nCWE_ID: CWE-918\nCWE_NAME: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)\n", "output_dir": "/home/medo/laMiaTesi/agentAnalysisDocker/outputs/polito_deepseek-v4/TandoorRecipes__recip