n the synchronous ZIP unpacker.\n\n4. **`crates/uv-extract/src/error.rs`** — Added two new error variants: `EmptyFilename` and `UnacceptableFilename { filename }` (with control characters replaced by U+FFFD `�`).\n\n5. **`Cargo.toml`** / **`Cargo.lock`** — Added the `regex` dependency and updated the `async_zip` git revision.\n\n6. **Test file** — Added `reject_invalid_archive_member_names` test using a crafted wheel `cbwheeldiff2==0.0.1` that contains a control character in a filename.\n\n### Security impact\n\n**Before the fix:** The existing code only rejected filenames containing null bytes (`\\0`), specifically to prevent directory traversal. All other control characters (escape, newline, carriage return, backspace, tab, etc.) were allowed to pass through unchecked.\n\n**The vulnerability:** A malicious wheel published to PyPI could embed filenames containing control characters. When `uv` processes such a wheel and displays an error message containing the filename (e.g. `Skipping unsafe file name: {path}` or any of the structured error messages in the `Error` enum), those control characters are interpreted by the user's terminal emulator. This enables:\n\n- **Terminal escape sequence injection** — An attacker can use ESC (`\\x1b`) sequences to hide real errors, create fake successful output, or manipulate terminal state (a form of UI redressing).\n- **Log forging** — Newline/carriage return characters can inject fake log entries, misleading the user about what happened.\n- **Backspace injection** — Backspace (`\\x08`) can erase parts of output to hide malicious content.\n\nSince `uv` is a package manager that downloads and installs packages from PyPI (an open ecosystem), any attacker can upload a malicious wheel, and a user running `uv pip install <attacker-package>` would be exposed. This is a realistic and exploitable attack vector.\n\n### CWE Classification\n\nThe most precise classification is **CWE-150: Improper Neutralization of Escape, Meta, or Control S