s SVG is uploaded and sanitized, the sanitizer library will attempt to **resolve and fetch the external URLs** referenced in the SVG. This is a classic **Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)** vector through SVG file uploads.\n\nAn attacker could craft an SVG like:\n```xml\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n  <image xlink:href=\"http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/\"/>\n</svg>\n```\nOn upload, the sanitizer would attempt to dereference `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/` (the AWS metadata endpoint), leaking cloud credentials.\n\n**Impact:** SSRF via SVG upload — an attacker can make the server issue arbitrary HTTP requests to internal or external systems.\n\n**CVSS-like severity:** High — External attacker can upload SVG via product reviews (unauthenticated) or search (unauthenticated), or via TinyMCE (authenticated admin).\n\n**CWE: CWE-918 (Server-Side Request Forgery)**\n\n---\n\n### Change 2: Installer Middleware — Authentication Bypass (Files 04, 05)\n\n**File:** `packages/Webkul/Installer/src/Http/Middleware/CanInstall.php`\n\nThis middleware is registered **globally** in `bootstrap/app.php` and also in middleware group `'install'`. It runs on every request.\n\n**Before:**\n```php\nif ($this->isAlreadyInstalled() && ! $request->ajax()) {\n    return redirect()->route('shop.home.index');\n}\n```\n\n**After:**\n```php\nif ($this->isAlreadyInstalled()) {\n    if (! $request->ajax()) {\n        return redirect()->route('shop.home.index');\n    }\n    return response()->json([\n        'message'=> trans('installer::app.installer.middleware.already-installed'),\n    ], 403);\n}\n```\n\n**Vulnerability:**\nThe old condition checked `$this->isAlreadyInstalled() && ! $request->ajax()`. When the application **was already installed** AND the request **was AJAX** (i.e., `$request->ajax()` returns `true`), the `! $request->ajax()` became `false`, making the entire condition `false`. This meant no redirect occu