rd_strength()` enforcing:\n- Minimum 8 characters\n- At least one uppercase letter\n- At least one digit\n- At least one special character\n\nThis prevents users (and attackers) from setting trivially weak passwords.\n\n### 3. CWE-613: Insufficient Session Expiration\n\nBefore the fix, when a password was changed, **all existing JWT sessions remained valid** until token expiration (up to 24 hours by default). The fix:\n- Adds a `password_changed_at` column to the `User` model (file_05, file_07)\n- Adds `iat` (issued-at) claim to JWT tokens (file_02)\n- During token refresh, checks if the token was issued before the last password change; if so, rejects the refresh\n\nThis ensures that after a password change (especially if done by an admin resetting a compromised account), active sessions from before the change cannot be extended.\n\n### 4. CWE-367: Time-of-check Time-of-use (TOCTOU) Race Condition + CWE-59: Improper Link Resolution Before File Access\n\nThe JWT secret file creation was vulnerable:\n\n```python\n# BEFORE:\nwith open(jwt_secret_file, \"w\") as f:\n    f.write(str(jwt_secret))\n```\n\nIf an attacker could create a symlink at `.jwt_secret` pointing to another file (e.g., `/etc/shadow` or a configuration file), the JWT secret would be written to that arbitrary file. The `\"w\"` mode would follow the symlink. The fix uses:\n\n```python\n# AFTER:\nfd = os.open(jwt_secret_file, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, 0o600)\nwith os.fdopen(fd, \"w\") as f:\n    f.write(str(jwt_secret))\n```\n\n`O_EXCL` ensures the file is created atomically and fails if it already exists (even as a symlink). The `0o600` permissions restrict access to the owner only, preventing other processes/users from reading the secret.\n\n### Summary\n\nThis commit is **unambiguously a security fix** addressing multiple weaknesses:\n\nVULNERABILITY_FOUND: yes\nCWE_ID: CWE-620, CWE-521, CWE-613, CWE-367, CWE-59\nCWE_NAME: Unverified Password Change, Weak Password Requirements, Insufficient