le (e.g., custom socket factories or deserialization triggers), an attacker may achieve remote code execution on the server.\n\n3. **Arbitrary file read** — The `IniFile` parameter itself can be used to read arbitrary files from the server filesystem.\n\nThis is a well-documented attack vector in JDBC connection manipulation research (e.g., CVE-2023-23457 and related PostgreSQL JDBC advisories).\n\n### Vulnerability Verdict\n\n**Yes, this is a security vulnerability fix.** It closes an incomplete blocklist that left a known dangerous JDBC parameter unblocked, enabling injection-style attacks against the Redshift JDBC driver.\n\n### CWE Classification\n\nThe most specific weakness is **CWE-184 (Incomplete List of Disallowed Inputs)** — the code uses a blocklist-based approach to filter dangerous JDBC parameters but omits `IniFile`, which is equally dangerous. The broader category **CWE-74 (Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Output Used by a Downstream Component)** also applies since user-controlled JDBC URL components are not neutralized and are passed to the downstream JDBC driver, allowing injection of the `IniFile` parameter. I list CWE-184 first as it is the more precise root cause.\n\n---\n\nVULNERABILITY_FOUND: yes\nCWE_ID: CWE-184, CWE-74\nCWE_NAME: Incomplete List of Disallowed Inputs, Improper Neutralization of Special Elements in Output Used by a Downstream Component ('Injection')\n", "output_dir": "/home/medo/laMiaTesi/agentAnalysisDocker/outputs/polito_deepseek-v4/dataease__dataease/8f1c21834a62/agent2", "created_at": "2026-08-07T12:38:01.087934+00:00"}
