the wrapped-around pointer — reading arbitrary memory.\n- In `decode`: `ptr[3], ptr[2], ptr[1], ptr[0]` (for fixed32 fields) and `ProtoLengthDelimited(ptr, field_length)` (for length-delimited fields) read attacker-controlled offsets from arbitrary memory locations.\n\n### Exploitability\n\nThis code processes incoming native API protobuf messages from the network. An attacker who can send messages to an ESPHome device (on the local network, or remotely if exposed) can craft a malicious protobuf message with a large length-delimited field size. This bypasses the bounds check, leads to out-of-bounds reads, and can leak sensitive data or cause a crash (DoS).\n\n### Classification\n\n| CWE ID | Name | Role |\n|--------|------|------|\n| **CWE-190** | Integer Overflow or Wraparound | Root cause — `ptr + field_length` wraps on 32-bit pointers |\n| **CWE-125** | Out-of-bounds Read | Consequence — reading memory outside the buffer after wraparound |\n\nBoth genuinely apply: CWE-190 describes *how* the check fails, and CWE-125 describes *what happens* as a result.\n\n---\nVULNERABILITY_FOUND: yes\nCWE_ID: CWE-190, CWE-125\nCWE_NAME: Integer Overflow or Wraparound, Out-of-bounds Read\n", "output_dir": "/home/medo/laMiaTesi/agentAnalysisDocker/outputs/polito_deepseek-v4/esphome__esphome/69d7b6e92103/agent2", "created_at": "2026-08-07T13:02:56.691231+00:00"}
