{"repo_name": "makeplane/plane", "repo_url": "https://github.com/makeplane/plane.git", "commit": "9070acbbe81bc02db5c169789da6862d5fc35d96", "agent": "agent1", "model": "polito/deepseek-v4", "status": "ok", "response": "## Analysis\n\nLet me break down both changes:\n\n### File 1 (`file_01`) — `ProjectAssetEndpoint.patch`\n**Before:** `FileAsset.objects.get(id=pk)` — fetches an asset purely by its primary key, with no check that the asset belongs to the workspace (`slug`) or project (`project_id`) provided in the URL.\n\n**After:** `FileAsset.objects.get(id=pk, workspace__slug=slug, project_id=project_id)` — scopes the query to the workspace and project from the URL.\n\nThis is a classic **Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR)**. An authenticated user (with `ROLE.ADMIN, ROLE.MEMBER, ROLE.GUEST`) in workspace A could modify an asset in workspace B by providing workspace B's asset ID (`pk`) while using workspace A's slug in the URL. The original code only verified the asset existed — it never verified the caller had any right to access that asset across workspace/project boundaries.\n\n### File 2 (`file_02`) — `IssueAttachmentEndpoint.delete`\n**Before:** `FileAsset.objects.get(pk=pk)` — fetches an attachment purely by primary key, ignoring the workspace, project, and issue context from the URL path.\n\n**After:** `FileAsset.objects.filter(pk=pk, workspace__slug=slug, project_id=project_id, issue_id=issue_id).first()` — properly scopes to all URL-level identifiers. Also added a 404 response if the scoped query returns nothing (instead of letting the `.get()` exception propagate or, in the original code, proceeding to delete an asset from a different context).\n\nDespite the `@allow_permission([ROLE.ADMIN], creator=True, model=FileAsset)` decorator checking admin role and creator status, the unscoped query meant an admin who created assets in multiple workspaces could manipulate attachments across workspace/project/issue boundaries by mixing URL parameters. The fix ensures the query respects the URL's hierarchical context (workspace → project → issue).\n\n### Conclusion\n\nBoth changes fix the same class of security vulnerability: **Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR)**. An attacker can manipulate a user-controlled identifier (`pk`/asset ID) in the URL to access or modify resources belonging to other workspaces or projects. The fix adds proper authorization scoping by including `workspace__slug`, `project_id`, and/or `issue_id` in the database query.\n\nCWE-639 is the most specific and accurate classification here — it is the canonical IDOR identifier for \"Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key.\"\n\nCWE-863 (Incorrect Authorization) is a broader parent category, but CWE-639 is more precise for this exact pattern.\n\nVULNERABILITY_FOUND: yes\nCWE_ID: CWE-639\nCWE_NAME: Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key\n", "output_dir": "/home/medo/laMiaTesi/agentAnalysisDocker/outputs/polito_deepseek-v4/makeplane__plane/9070acbbe81b/agent1", "created_at": "2026-08-07T14:07:47.045937+00:00"}
