{"repo_name": "notepad-plus-plus/wingup", "repo_url": "https://github.com/notepad-plus-plus/wingup.git", "commit": "ce0037549995ed0396cc363544d14b3425614fdb", "agent": "agent3", "model": "polito/deepseek-v4", "status": "ok", "response": "Based on my thorough analysis of the codebase, I have identified a significant security vulnerability.\n\n## Detailed Analysis\n\n**WinGUp for Notepad++** is a Windows auto-updater that downloads and extracts plugin packages from ZIP archives. The core logic lives in `/work/src/winmain.cpp`.\n\n### Vulnerability Found: Zip Slip — Path Traversal via Malicious ZIP Extraction (CWE-22)\n\nThe `decompress()` function (lines 384–502) extracts ZIP archives without validating entry paths. When a ZIP entry is read:\n\n```cpp\nwstring file2extrait = s2ws(entry->GetFullName());  // line 411\nwstring extraitFullFilePath = unzipDestTo;           // line 412\nPathAppend(extraitFullFilePath, file2extrait);       // line 413 — no path validation\n```\n\nThe entry's `GetFullName()` is taken directly from the ZIP central directory header and appended to the destination path with the custom `PathAppend()` function — a simple concatenation (`strDest += L\"\\\\\" + str2append`) that performs **no sanitization** of `..` or absolute-path components.\n\nThe underlying ZipLib library (`/work/src/ZipLib/ZipArchiveEntry.cpp`) does **not** guard against this either:\n- `IsValidFilename()` (lines 24–39) only checks that the filename is not empty or made solely of `/` characters — it does **not** reject `..` components.\n- `SetFullName()` (lines 146–176) normalizes slashes and removes leading `/`, but never strips or rejects directory-traversal sequences like `../`.\n\n**Attack scenario**: If an attacker controls or MITMs the download URL (e.g., via a compromised update server or a network position), they can serve a ZIP archive containing entries such as:\n```\n../../AppData/Local/Temp/evil.dll\n../../Windows/system32/malicious.dll\n```\nThese would be extracted outside the intended plugin directory, achieving **arbitrary file write**. This is a classic **Zip Slip** — CWE-22.\n\nThe vulnerability is exploitable in the plugin-update path (lines 1289–1411), where `gup.exe -unzip` downloads a ZIP from a configured URL and extracts it. The only integrity check is a SHA-256 hash passed on the command line — and the hash is provided by the same caller (Notepad++), which gets it from the same potentially-compromised server, so it offers no protection against a compromise at the source.\n\n### Other Observations (less impactful, not primary findings)\n\n- **HTTP Parameter Injection (CWE-88)**: In `getUpdateInfo()` (line 952–963), `customParam` is concatenated directly into the URL without encoding, e.g. `urlComplete += L\"&param=\" + customParam`. An attacker controlling the `-p` argument could inject additional query parameters.\n- **Weak domain prefix check (CWE-20)**: The `-d` forceDomain check (lines 1479–1481) is a simple string prefix comparison — `https://good.com` would match `https://good.com.evil.com`.\n- **Certificate revocation disabled**: `CURLSSLOPT_NO_REVOKE` (lines 853, 997) disables CRL/OCSP checks.\n\nThe Zip Slip is the most severe and actionable vulnerability.\n\n---\n\nVULNERABILITY_FOUND: yes\nCWE_ID: CWE-22\nCWE_NAME: Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory ('Path Traversal')\n", "output_dir": "/home/medo/laMiaTesi/agentAnalysisDocker/outputs/polito_deepseek-v4/notepad-plus-plus__wingup/ce0037549995/agent3", "created_at": "2026-08-07T14:51:55.507658+00:00"}
{"repo_name": "ntpsec/gpsd", "repo_url": "https://github.com/ntpsec/gpsd.git", "commit": "dc966aa74c075d0a6535811d98628625cbfbe3f4", "agent": "agent1", "model": "polito/deepseek-v4", "status": "ok", "response": "Now let me read the rest of the after.txt to see the remaining code:\n## Analysis Complete\n\nI have carefully reviewed the before.txt, after.txt, and diff.txt for file_01. This is a C source file from the **GPSD project** — specifically the NMEA2000 over CAN driver (`driver_nmea2000.c`).\n\nThe vast majority of changes are **cosmetic**: comment style conversions (`/* */` → `//`), whitespace and spacing adjustments in `for` loops, repositioning of comment paragraphs, and removal of a copyright year. These have no security impact.\n\nOne single function, however, receives a **substantive, security-critical change**: `hnd_129540()` — the handler for **PGN 129540 (GNSS Satellites in View)**.\n\n### The Vulnerability\n\nThe value `satellites_visible` is extracted directly from an untrusted CAN bus frame byte (`bu[2]`). In the original code:\n\n1. No upper bound check is performed on `satellites_visible` before using it as the loop limit.\n2. The loop iterates `l1 < satellites_visible` and writes to `session->gpsdata.skyview[l1]` — an array of size `MAXCHANNELS`.\n3. It also reads from `bu[3+12*l1+...]` — if `satellites_visible` implies a message longer than the actual `len`, this reads out of bounds from the receive buffer.\n\nA specially crafted CAN frame with a large `satellites_visible` value causes:\n- **Out-of-bounds write** into `skyview[]` beyond its allocated size (CWE-787)\n- **Out-of-bounds read** from `bu` past the valid buffer length (CWE-125)\n\n### The Fix\n\nThe commit adds two guards:\n\n1. **Cap to MAXCHANNELS**: If `satellites_visible >= MAXCHANNELS`, it logs a warning and caps the value to `MAXCHANNELS`. The comment explicitly states: *\"Handle a CVE for overrunning skyview[]\"*.\n\n2. **Length validation**: It computes `expected_len = 3 + (12